/** Procedures an agent can follow, offered as prompts and as resources. */
export const PLAYBOOKS: readonly Playbook[] = [
  { name: "buyer-pitch", description: "Use when the user wants to get a product onto a retailer's shelf or pitch a buyer — \"get me in front of the beverage buyer\", \"pitch the buyer\", \"how do I get into Kroger\", \"help me get listed\", \"write a sell sheet\", \"what do I tell the category buyer\". Produces the opening on the shelf (which chains carry the category, who leads it, where the user's price lands on the ladder) and a short pitch that quotes those figures. NOT for placing an existing brand's prices alone (that is position-on-shelf) and NOT for auditing where a brand is already carried (that is retail-coverage).", text: "A buyer decides on THEIR shelf: what it carries, who leads it, and whether a new item fills a gap\nor just duplicates what is there. A pitch built only from a price tier answers none of that, so this\nanswer needs the shelf AND the price, from the governed tools, before a word of the pitch is written.\n\n## The procedure\n\n**Step 1 — the category.** Resolve the product's category with `ontology_resolve` and pass the\nshelf's `key` (or its `spt`/`gpc` id) to the tools below. When it cannot resolve, pass the plain\ncategory name: the tools answer with candidate keys to retry with. A missing key is never a reason to\nask the user.\n\n**Step 2 — the shelf the buyer runs.** Call BOTH:\n- `retailer_assortment` on the category (no brand): the chains that carry it, how many products each\n  lists and at what median price. When the user names a retailer, that chain's row is the buyer's shelf;\n  otherwise the top chains are where to pitch first.\n- `share_of_shelf` on the category: who leads the shelf, so the pitch can say what it sits beside and\n  what it is not a copy of. Its `retailer` is a tracked platform (amazon, walmart), not a chain: pass one\n  only when the named retailer is one, and omit it otherwise.\n\n**Step 3 — where the price lands.** With a price in the question, `price_bands` on the category with\nno brand: which tier it falls in and who sits either side. With a brand and no price, follow\nposition-on-shelf Steps 1 to 4 instead.\n\n**Step 4 — the pitch.** Lead with the opening (the chain to pitch and why: it carries the category,\nand its shelf is thin or led by one brand at the tier the product enters), then the product's place on\nthat shelf with the one or two figures that prove it, then the ask (a test listing, a promo window).\nKeep it to a few sentences.\n\n## What makes this answer wrong\n\n**Only a price.** A tier with no chain named is a pricing answer, not a pitch. If `retailer_assortment`\nor `share_of_shelf` came back empty or thin, say so and name what you did find; do not skip them.\n\n**An invented buyer.** Never write a buyer's name, email, phone number or profile link. Address the\npitch to the role (\"the category buyer, Beverages\"). No tool here returns a buyer's contact: say so in\none sentence if the user asks for it.\n\n**Per-unit maths without its source.** A per-can price is fine when it comes from a pack price you\nquote (the user's \"$24.99 a 12-pack\" is $2.08 a can): write the pack price and the count with it.\n" },
  { name: "category-brief", description: "Use when the user asks for a broad standing read on a category rather than one metric — \"brief me on my category\", \"what's happening in X\", \"give me today's summary\", \"catch me up on the category\", \"daily rundown\". Produces one connected brief covering price level, biggest movers, promo activity and new launches, and mentions it can be saved as a daily agent. NOT for a single specific question — if the ask names one metric, answer that directly instead of running a brief.", text: "Three questions in one, answered as ONE connected read rather than three stacked paragraphs. The\nfailure mode here is not misrouting, it is sprawl: a dozen speculative calls, each of which costs\nmoney.\n\n## The procedure\n\nRun these as direct sequential calls, and stop when a lane has nothing to say.\n\n**Price level** — `category_summary` for the category: product and brand counts, the price spread,\nin-stock rate. This is also your coverage check for everything below.\n\n**Movers** — `price_change_leaders` over the recent window, or `inflation_tracker` for a longer read.\nLike-for-like only.\n\n**Promo activity** — `promo_intelligence`. Report frequency and depth, and respect a withheld rate.\n\n**Then connect them.** The value of a brief is the link between lanes — movers that coincide with a\npromo wave, launches concentrated in one tier. Four disconnected lists is not a brief.\n\n## Coverage governs the whole thing\n\nLead with what the data supports. If the category has thin coverage, say that FIRST and keep the\nbrief short, rather than producing four hedged paragraphs that each sound like a finding. A brief\nwhose every line is qualified is worse than three sentences that are solid.\n\n## What makes this answer wrong\n\n**Fanning out speculatively.** Every lane is one call. If a lane returns nothing, say so in a clause\nand move on — do not retry it three ways.\n\n**Burying the headline.** Lead with the single most important thing in the category today, then the\nsupporting lanes. A brief that opens with product counts has buried it.\n" },
  { name: "diagnose-price-move", description: "Use when the user asks what changed on price and why — \"what moved most on price\", \"why did prices go up\", \"biggest movers this month\", \"did prices drop because of promotions\", \"category inflation\". Produces the movers with their like-for-like change, the rising/falling split, and a promo read alongside so a discount wave is not mistaken for repricing. NOT for a static snapshot of where prices sit today (that is position-on-shelf or a category summary).", text: "Answer two things together, because either alone misleads: WHAT moved, and WHETHER promotions drove\nit. A list of movers with no promo read invites the reader to conclude a brand repriced when it ran\na week of discounts.\n\n## The procedure\n\n**Step 1 — the movement, like-for-like.**\n`inflation_tracker` for a category-level read over a quarter or longer, or `price_change_leaders` for\nper-product movers over a short window. Both compare MATCHED products between two bands. Never\ndescribe a change as first-versus-last snapshots — a basket that gains one expensive product\n\"inflates\" without a single price moving, and that is the metric the canon bans.\n\n**Step 2 — the split, not just the headline.**\nReport rising / stable / falling alongside the change. A category can be flat on average while most\nproducts fell and two rose sharply; the headline alone hides that.\n\n**Step 3 — the promo overlay.**\n`promo_intelligence` over the same window. If promo frequency or depth jumped alongside the price\nmove, the honest reading is promotion, not repricing. Say which one you think it is, hedged.\n\n**Step 4 — respect the matched basket.**\nBelow the matched-basket floor the change is withheld rather than reported from a thin basket. When\nit is withheld, say so and give the matched count. Do NOT substitute a different metric to have a\nnumber to quote — an unavailable change is a real answer.\n\n## What makes this answer wrong\n\n**Quoting a naive change.** Some responses carry both a matched and a naive figure, and the naive\none is the change-of-average the canon bans. Use the like-for-like number; if only the naive one is\npresent, treat the change as unavailable.\n\n**Attributing cause as fact.** Promotion, cost pressure and mix shift all look the same in a price\nseries. Name the most likely driver as a hypothesis (\"a likely driver could be\"), never as a finding.\n\n**A window the user did not ask for.** Inflation is a quarter-and-up question; a 30-day ask belongs\nto price_change_leaders. State the window you actually used.\n" },
  { name: "position-on-shelf", description: "Use when the user asks where a brand sits on price against its shelf — \"where am I priced\", \"which tier do we play in\", \"how do we compare to competitors on price\", \"place us on the price ladder\", \"who sits above and below us\". Produces the tier the brand occupies, its median against the shelf's, and the neighbours either side, rendered as the price-architecture histogram with the brand's catalogue stacked on the market distribution. NOT for \"what moved on price recently\" (that is diagnose-price-move) and NOT for discovering who the competitors ARE (that is competitor-research).", text: "Place the brand on its shelf's own price ladder and say who is next to it. The answer a category\nmanager wants is a TIER and a neighbour list, not an average — $249 is luxury on the earbuds shelf\nand entry on laptops, so an average price across a category answers nothing.\n\n## The procedure\n\n**Step 0 — no brand, but a price.** With no brand in focus or in the question but a price the user\ngave (\"we sell our 12-pack at $24.99, where are we priced?\"), skip Steps 1 to 3: call `price_bands` on\nthe category with no brand, then say which tier that price falls in and who sits either side. With\nneither a brand nor a price there is nothing to place: ask them to name their brand. When the ask is\nto pitch a buyer or get onto a retailer's shelf, the price is only part of it: use the buyer-pitch prompt.\n\n**Step 1 — find where the brand actually has retail density.**\n`brand_breakdown` on the focus brand FIRST. A brand's workspace categories are what it *says* it\nsells; brand_breakdown is where it is actually priced. Use the densest real category, not every\nfocus category, and not the first one in the list.\n\n**Step 2 — position it there.**\n`price_positioning` on that category (pass the shelf: without one the route compares the brand\nwith the whole catalogue and returns no `tier`). This returns `price_index` (brand average over\ncategory average) and, when the shelf publishes a price band, `tier`. Do not fan out to every peer:\npeer positioning is useful only when the peer set is small and known.\n\n**Step 3 — stop when peers come back empty.**\nWhen a peer returns n=0 or insufficient coverage, STOP. Do not thrash `competitive_landscape`, and\nnever search the web for retail prices — no external tool returns them.\n\n**Step 4 — the ladder itself.**\n`price_bands` with the brand named. It returns the shelf's tier edges and, per tier, how many of the\nbrand's products sit there (`tiers[].brand_products`), which is what draws the focused histogram.\nScope it by the shelf's key (`electronics/audio/audio-components/headphones-headsets/headphones`) or by\nthe `spt` id `ontology_resolve` returned for it, not by a display label. When the name has\nco-equal shelves, never pick one silently: run each and say so.\n\n## What makes this answer wrong\n\n**Reading a tier as a ratio.** Tiers are equal-population by construction: \"premium\" means the\nbrand's median sits in the shelf's fourth fifth, a fact about THIS shelf. It is not\nbrand-average-over-category-average. Do not describe a tier as if it were a price index, or vice\nversa.\n\n**An inherited ladder read as exact.** A thin shelf borrows its band width from its own product type\nor from its parent, and is re-centred on its own median. `banded_from` says when, and from what: a\n`type:` value is the product type's band, and anything else is a parent shelf. Treat those edges as\napproximate and say so, naming the product type or the shelf, never the raw `type:` key.\n\n**Quoting a neighbour that is not on this shelf.** \"Above and below\" means on the same ladder. A\nbrand from a different category is not a neighbour however similar its price.\n" },
  { name: "promo-pressure", description: "Use when the user asks who is discounting and how hard — \"who is discounting hardest\", \"promo pressure in my category\", \"how often and how deep are competitors discounting\", \"are we being undercut on promotions\", \"promo activity this month\". Produces a ranking by promo frequency and depth against the category's own baseline, with an explicit flag when list-price coverage is too thin to trust. NOT for whether a price CHANGE was promo-driven (that is diagnose-price-move).", text: "Rank who discounts hardest, on two axes that are routinely conflated: how OFTEN (frequency) and how\nDEEP (average and max depth). A brand discounting 5% every week and one discounting 40% twice a\nquarter are different problems, and a single \"promo rate\" hides which you are looking at.\n\n## The procedure\n\n**Step 1 — the ranking.**\n`promo_intelligence` for the category. `aggregate_by` chooses the axis: `brand` for \"who is\ndiscounting\", `retailer` for \"where discounting happens\". Pick the one the question asks for; do not\nreport brands when the user asked about channels.\n\n**Step 2 — against the baseline.**\nA brand at 30% promo frequency means nothing alone. Compare it to the category's own level, which is\nwhat makes this an audit rather than a list.\n\n**Step 3 — check list-price coverage before trusting any of it.**\nA promo rate needs a list price to compare against, and most rows do not have one. Where coverage is\nthin the rate is WITHHELD rather than reported, because counting products with no list price as\n\"not on promo\" understates it systematically. When it is withheld, say that the data cannot support\na promo read for that slice — never present the absence as \"little promotional activity\".\n\n## What makes this answer wrong\n\n**Reading a withheld rate as a low rate.** These are opposite claims. \"We cannot measure this\" is\nnot \"they are not discounting.\"\n\n**Comparing frequency to depth.** They are different units. \"Discounting hardest\" usually means\ndepth; \"under constant promotion\" means frequency. Say which you ranked on.\n\n**Treating a group count as a product count.** The ranking is one row per brand or retailer, each\nalready an aggregate over many observations. A five-brand ranking is not five products.\n" },
  { name: "retail-coverage", description: "Use when the user asks where a brand is or is not carried — \"which retailers carry us\", \"where are my competitors listed but I'm not\", \"distribution gaps\", \"what stores stock this brand\", \"retail coverage vs peers\". Produces the chains carrying the brand today and the shelves peers are on that it is missing from, named as real store chains a buyer can act on. NOT for prices AT those retailers (that is position-on-shelf) and NOT for how often they discount (that is promo-pressure).", text: "Answer in STORE CHAINS, because that is what a distribution gap means to whoever has to close it.\nThe one thing that makes this answer useless is reporting scrape lanes as if they were retailers.\n\n## The axis, and why it matters\n\n`products.platform` is the LANE the data arrived on, not the chain that sells the product. The\nshopify lane alone carries 214 distinct storefronts. Grouping by platform reports \"49 retailers\" for\na category that has 99 real ones, and names none of them usefully.\n\nUse the tools that resolve the seller CHAIN — `retailer_assortment`, `share_of_shelf`,\n`competitive_landscape`. They already collapse the lane onto the chain axis.\n\n## The procedure\n\n**Step 1 — where the brand is.**\n`retailer_assortment` for the focus brand: the chains carrying it and how much of its catalogue each\none holds. Depth matters as much as presence — being in a chain with two SKUs is not distribution.\n\n**Step 2 — where the peers are.**\nThe same read for the competitive set, or `share_of_shelf` for the category. The GAP is the\ninteresting part: chains carrying peers where the focus brand is absent.\n\n**Step 3 — name the chains.**\nReport store names. \"Three retailers\" is not actionable; \"Kroger, Publix and Wegmans\" is.\n\n**Step 4 — absence is scoped.**\nThe brand may be carried somewhere we do not look. Say \"not seen at X\" only when X is among the\nchains we scanned on the brand's shelves: `ontology_neighbors` with `relations: not_observed_at`\nlists exactly those, and `ontology_coverage` with `retailer` and the brand's id answers one chain\n(`absence: not_observed` is the only absence you may claim; `unscanned` means nothing can be said).\nAnywhere else, say \"not present in tracked coverage\" rather than \"not carried\" — the second is a claim\nabout the world, the first about our data.\n\n## What makes this answer wrong\n\n**An unknown channel filter answered as an empty shelf.** If a channel or retailer filter is not\nrecognised, the tools return an explicit note listing the real ones. Act on that note and retry with\na real chain — never present the empty result as \"no coverage\".\n\n**Counting listings as distribution.** One SKU in a chain is a listing. Report the depth alongside\nthe presence so the two are not confused.\n" },
];

/** The six rules for using the ontology tools, offered as one always-visible resource. */
export const RULES: Playbook = { name: "ontology-rules", description: "The six rules for using the ontology tools. Read them once; they apply to every question that names a brand, category, product type, retailer or product.", text: "Six rules, in the order a question uses them.\n\n1. **Resolve before you query.** A brand, category, product type, retailer or product named in a\n   question is resolved once per conversation with `ontology_resolve`, and every later call is scoped to\n   what came back (an id, or the category's key), never to a guessed string. One concept per term:\n   \"woman-owned candy\" is two terms. Pass `within` when a name is a homonym or the shelf is already\n   known. A `miss` goes to the user with its candidates; it is never silently narrowed.\n2. **Matches are co-equal.** A name that fits two shelves (\"protein bars\") or two brands (\"gerber\")\n   returns both, none ranked best, and the order of the list is fixed, not a ranking. Never pick one\n   silently: run each and say so, or ask.\n3. **Check coverage before you hedge or spend.** `ontology_coverage` on the candidate scopes decides\n   the grain (shelf or parent, type or shelf) and the wording: \"on a thin sample\" is a number, not a\n   mood.\n4. **Use the neighbourhood for nuance.** `ontology_neighbors`: siblings for \"versus the shelf\", the\n   shelves a type is filed under for \"also filed under\", `footprint` for where a brand actually sells.\n5. **Query by id, and say what you resolved.** The paid routes that take a category or a brand also\n   take `spt` (a shelf), `gpc` (a product type, across every shelf it is filed under) and `brand_id`.\n   Send an id instead of the name-shaped parameter, never with it, and state the scope the answer is\n   about. The `suggested_next_calls` in each free answer are ready-to-run calls with ids filled in and\n   the price beside each: advice, not a route you must take.\n6. **Absence is scoped.** Say \"not seen at X\" only when X is in `retailers_scanned` for that shelf, and\n   \"no in-store price evidence\" when `physical_store` is in `channels_not_observed`, never \"no in-store\n   promos\". Everything else is unknown, not absent.\n" };
